h the opening of Fiona Stanley Hospital in Murdoch on 3 February 2015 it was substantially downsized, with the closure of its emergency department and a range of other services being relocated to the new hospital.","wpu":"Fremantle Hospital","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/AUS_Perth%2C_Fremantle%2C_South_Terrace_001.jpg/960px-AUS_Perth%2C_Fremantle%2C_South_Terrace_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Laundry","g":"essentials","k":"laundry","la":-32.052348,"lo":115.755918,"d":1219},{"n":"Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":-32.062064,"lo":115.756834,"d":1647},{"n":"Mana Wholefoods & Café","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":-32.065774,"lo":115.75258,"d":1683,"h":"Mo-Su 08:00-18:00"},{"n":"Spare Parts Puppet Theatre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.05315,"lo":115.74514,"d":202,"ds":"The Spare Parts Puppet Theatre is located at 1–9 Short Street, Fremantle, Western Australia, in Pioneer Park, opposite the Fremantle railway station.","wpu":"Spare Parts Puppet Theatre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/WTFr_Nov_2011_gnangarra-37.jpg/960px-WTFr_Nov_2011_gnangarra-37.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Higham's Buildings","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.054639,"lo":115.746389,"d":367,"ds":"Higham's Buildings is a heritage listed building located at 101 High Street, on the corner of Market Street in the Fremantle West End Heritage area. It is one of many commercial buildings constructed in Fremantle during the gold boom period in the late nineteenth and early twentieth century. The two storey building was constructed in 1890 in the Federation Free Classical style.","wpu":"Higham's Buildings","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Highams_Building_corner_of_High_and_Market_Street_in_1933_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Highams_Building_corner_of_High_and_Market_Street_in_1933_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fremantle Town Hall","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.054293,"lo":115.7479,"d":483,"ds":"Fremantle Town Hall is a town hall located in the portside city of Fremantle, Western Australia, and situated on the corner of High, William and Adelaide Streets. The official opening, on 22 June 1887, coincided with the celebration of Queen Victoria's Jubilee and it was formally named by the mayor, Daniel Keen Congdon and the state governor, Frederick Broome, as the Town and Jubilee Hall.","wpu":"Fremantle Town Hall","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/FremantleTownHall1_gobeirne.jpg/960px-FremantleTownHall1_gobeirne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St Patrick's Basilica, Fremantle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.0509,"lo":115.75,"d":699,"ds":"The Basilica of Saint Patrick is a Roman Catholic church located on Adelaide Street in Fremantle, Western Australia. Pope John Paul II raised the shrine to the status of Minor Basilica via his Pontifical decree Hoc Anno on 20 July 1994. The decree was signed and notarised by Cardinal Angelo Sodano.","wpu":"St Patrick's Basilica, Fremantle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/St_Patrick%27s_Basilica%2C_Fremantle.jpg/960px-St_Patrick%27s_Basilica%2C_Fremantle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Challenger Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.06,"lo":115.74,"d":828,"ds":"Challenger Harbour is a marina in Fremantle, Western Australia adjacent to the Fremantle Fishing Boat Harbour. The harbour breakwater covers the historical site of the Fremantle Long Jetty.","wpu":"Challenger Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Aerial_view_of_Fremantle.JPG/960px-Aerial_view_of_Fremantle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fremantle Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.05,"lo":115.733333,"d":970,"ds":"Fremantle Harbour is Western Australia's largest and busiest general cargo port and an important historical site. The inner harbour handles a large volume of sea containers, vehicle imports and livestock exports, cruise shipping and naval visits, and operates 24 hours a day. It is located adjacent to the city of Fremantle, in the Perth metropolitan region.","wpu":"Fremantle Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Aerial_view_of_Fremantle.JPG/960px-Aerial_view_of_Fremantle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fremantle Fishing Boat Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.062031,"lo":115.745825,"d":1038,"ds":"Fremantle Fishing Boat Harbour is a marina in Fremantle, Western Australia adjacent to the more recently constructed Challenger Harbour. It provides large sheltered mooring areas and wharf space for vessels up to 60 metres (200 ft), refuelling facilities and support services to the Fremantle fishing industry.","wpu":"Fremantle Fishing Boat Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Fremantle_fishing_boat_harbour_gnangarra_1.JPG/960px-Fremantle_fishing_boat_harbour_gnangarra_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fremantle Arts Centre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.047469,"lo":115.754137,"d":1216,"ds":"The Fremantle Arts Centre is historic building complex on Ord Street in Fremantle, Western Australia. The heritage-listed building complex was built using convict labour between 1861 and 1868 and was used as a psychiatric hospital, initially called the Fremantle Lunatic Asylum, and later known as the Asylum for the Criminally Insane.","wpu":"Fremantle Arts Centre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/AU-fremantle-artscentre.jpg/960px-AU-fremantle-artscentre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Perth (Nyungar: Boorloo) is the capital and largest city of the state of Western Australia. It is the fourth-most-populous city in Australia, with a population of over 2.2 million in the city and 2.3 million within Greater Perth as of 2023. The world's most isolated city by certain criteria, Perth is part of the South West Land Division of Western Australia, with most of Perth's metropolitan area on the Swan Coastal Plain between the Indian Ocean and the Darling Scarp.","wpu":"Perth","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Perth_CBD_skyline_from_State_War_Memorial_Lookout%2C_2023%2C_04_b.jpg/960px-Perth_CBD_skyline_from_State_War_Memorial_Lookout%2C_2023%2C_04_b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}